WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.193
What shapes do complex equations draw in
the plane? You can find distances and

00:04.193 --> 00:08.385
angles in the plane. In this lesson we
read geometric loci from complex

00:08.385 --> 00:12.578
equations. By the end, Read circles,
perpendicular bisectors and rays from

00:12.578 --> 00:16.771
equations.

00:16.771 --> 00:21.103
The equation modulus of z minus two plus i
equals two describes a circle centred at

00:21.103 --> 00:25.435
two plus i with radius two.

00:25.435 --> 00:30.129
Modulus of z minus a equals modulus of z
minus b describes the perpendicular

00:30.129 --> 00:34.824
bisector of the segment joining a and b.

00:34.824 --> 00:39.615
The argument of z minus one minus i equals
sixty degrees describes a ray starting at

00:39.615 --> 00:44.405
one minus i at an angle of sixty degrees.

00:44.405 --> 00:49.185
Three loci to remember: a fixed distance
gives a circle; equal distances give a

00:49.185 --> 00:53.965
perpendicular bisector; a fixed argument
gives a ray.

00:53.965 --> 00:59.001
A common mistake is to treat the argument
equation as a full line. Pause and decide

00:59.001 --> 01:04.037
whether that is right. The argument
equation describes only a ray.

01:04.037 --> 01:09.287
Your turn. Describe the locus modulus of z
minus two plus i equals two. Pause the

01:09.287 --> 01:14.536
video. Hint: It is a fixed distance from a
fixed point.

01:14.536 --> 01:18.505
A circle centred at two plus i with radius
two. In a new context, Equidistance from

01:18.505 --> 01:22.474
one and minus i gives a perpendicular
bisector. Rate your confidence from one to

01:22.474 --> 01:26.443
five.

01:26.443 --> 01:30.263
Read the geometry first, then write the
equation. That is the key to loci. Next,

01:30.263 --> 01:34.083
we move into vector algebra.
